from pathlib import Path
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
import html
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'examples/meddecider-guide.html'
s=BeautifulSoup(p.read_text(),'html.parser')
def table(headers, rows):
 return '<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</th>' for x in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</td>' for x in r)+'</tr>' for r in rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'
def inject(chapter, heading, body):
 sec=s.select('.chapter')[chapter-1]
 frag=BeautifulSoup('<h3>'+heading+'</h3>'+body,'html.parser')
 sec.append(frag)
source='<p class="muted">Source: <a href="https://www.thelion.ai/projects/meddecider/">MedDecider primary project page</a>; numbers are developer-reported, not independently reproduced.</p>'
inject(3,'Exact public interface and boundaries', '''<p><code>MedDecider.decide(state, question, options, qtype="choice")</code> accepts a text or JSON state (or <code>None</code>), a plain-language question, and between two and ten answer options. The three types—<code>choice</code>, <code>noul</code>, and <code>score</code>—select different calibration temperatures; they do not select different neural heads. The reference loader reads the base model name from <code>meddecider_config.json</code>, applies the PEFT LoRA adapter, merges it, and disables reasoning for the chat-template prompt. The next-token letter scores are extracted without sampling.</p><p>Important implementation caveat: tokenization of A–J must be checked for the particular chat template. The provided educational NumPy lab starts after the vocabulary-logit readout, so it does not claim to reproduce the original model weights or tokenizer behavior.</p>'''+source)
inject(6,'Published calibration settings', table(['Variant','Choice temperature','Yes/no temperature','Ordered-score temperature'],[['4B','1.33','1.0','1.0'],['9B','1.43','1.0','1.0'],['27B','1.23','1.0','1.0'],['31B','1.49','1.0','1.0']])+'''<p>The published choice temperatures were fitted on in-distribution calibration data. A global temperature adjusts sharpness, not the ranking of choices in a single pass. The 31B reports ECE 0.029 over 12 unseen datasets, but the other model cards do not report comparable ECE values. A local deployment must recalibrate using local labeled examples.</p>'''+source)
inject(7,'Exact training hyperparameters',table(['Parameter','Reported value'],[['Epochs','1'],['Learning rate','1e-4'],['Warm-up','3% of steps'],['Schedule','Cosine decay to 10% of starting LR'],['Objective','Cross-entropy on offered option-letter logits'],['Answer positions','Shuffled every training example'],['LoRA target','All linear layers'],['LoRA rank','32 (4B/9B); 16 (27B/31B)']])+'''<p>With temperature T, the correct gradient for a letter logit is <code>(p_i − 1[i=y]) / T</code>. The simplified earlier example used T=1. The LoRA update changes the underlying projection weights via low-rank matrices, rather than fitting a newly attached classifier head.</p>'''+source)
inject(8,'Training corpus ledger',table(['4B mixture source','Decisions'],[['MedMCQA','40,000'],['MedQA','10,200'],['DDXPlus','12,000'],['Code-generated clinical notes','24,000'],['Robustness','9,000'],['General decisions','23,400']])+'''<p>The project reports a 4B run of 119,034 decisions and 18.8 million tokens. The displayed category counts sum to 118,600, which differs by 434; preserve the source's numbers and flag the discrepancy rather than silently making them match. The 9B, 27B and 31B use a different 35,800-decision mix, including evidence inference corpora and 6,000 synthetic trial minimal pairs. That smaller mix is more clinically targeted, so the variants are <em>not</em> a controlled parameter-only scaling experiment.</p>'''+source)
inject(9,'Clinical applications and action aggregation', '''<p><b>Trial pre-screening:</b> a fictional study uses three inclusion and two exclusion criteria. Each criterion is a separate three-option decision: meets, does not meet, or insufficient evidence. The tutorial uses a 0.8 threshold to invite for <em>full screening</em>, exclude, or send to a coordinator—not to enroll patients automatically.</p><p><b>Medication reconciliation:</b> one drug can be described as both discontinued and an allergy. The source reports an amoxicillin example with probabilities 0.466 and 0.525 for those two mutually exclusive response options. Since both imply “not currently taking,” grouping those actions produces a reported aggregate probability of 0.994 after including the small on-hold probability. Summing is appropriate only for disjoint answer categories that map to the same action.</p><p><b>Evidence classification:</b> trial statements can be supported, contradicted or absent from the source. Abstract screening is proposed, not benchmark-validated. The published workflows use synthetic records and have not been clinically validated.</p>'''+source)
inject(10,'Model configurations and compute',table(['Model','Base','LoRA rank / adapter','Training data','Reported fine-tuning compute'],[['4B (4.7B)','Qwen3.5-4B','32 / 130 MB','119,034 decisions','64 min, one GPU'],['9B (9.7B)','Qwen3.5-9B','32 / 173 MB','35,800 decisions','35 min, one H100'],['27B (27.8B)','Qwen3.8-27B','16 / 234 MB','35,800 decisions','85 min, one H200'],['31B (31.3B)','Gemma-4-31b-it','16 / 267 MB','35,800 decisions','93 min, one H200']])+'''<p>Duo averages the calibrated probabilities from the 27B and 31B and requires both to fit in accelerator memory. A larger model is not universally better: for tasks grounded in the provided note, smaller variants remain competitive; for questions requiring outside medical knowledge, the 31B scores higher on unseen exams.</p>'''+source)
inject(11,'Full family benchmark matrix',table(['Model','Unseen exams','Unseen clinical','Robustness','NLI4CT macro-F1','Polish LEK'],[['Duo','69.6%','89.5%','89.8%','0.845','89.2%'],['31B','70.2%','88.4%','88.8%','0.843','87.7%'],['27B','64.4%','89.7%','88.3%','0.827','87.1%'],['9B','57.5%','89.3%','82.9%','0.799','77.9%'],['4B','54.7%','87.8%','79.7%','0.776','not tested']])+'''<p>All models were evaluated on 9,849 held-out items, according to the project. The NLI4CT comparison is not evidence of a clear win over Jev: the paired difference is reported as +0.003, with a 95% confidence interval from −0.007 to +0.012. Because that interval contains zero, the project treats the results as a tie. The 31B also trails other open models on some unseen clinical-task and NLI4CT comparisons.</p>'''+source)
inject(12,'Measured injection failures',table(['Model','Follows planted instruction (lower is better)'],[['MedDecider-4B','5.4%'],['MedDecider-9B','2.0%'],['MedDecider-27B','3.0%'],['MedDecider-31B','2.7%'],['Jev 1.13','13.7%']])+'''<p>The test places misleading instructions in notes or trial records, using attack templates not seen during training. A low failure rate is not zero: model probabilities can still be redirected by untrusted text. Treat source notes as data, not authority, and use clinical review for consequential decisions.</p>'''+source)
inject(13,'Reproduction checklist', '''<ol><li>Choose the published base model and check its separate license.</li><li>Load the correct PEFT adapter, merge it, and apply the matching chat template with reasoning disabled.</li><li>Build the state/question/A–J option prompt and verify option-letter token IDs.</li><li>Extract final-position letter logits, apply the question-type temperature, and softmax only over offered letters.</li><li>Score reversed options, remap by semantic option, and average the two distributions.</li><li>Validate normalization, reversal invariance on symmetric examples, ECE, order sensitivity, and latency.</li><li>Recalibrate on local labeled data; evaluate abstention and harmful false positives before any operational use.</li></ol><p>The included NumPy lab implements steps 4–5 without loading weights. Reproducing the reported fine-tuning requires access to compatible training data, model weights, adequate compute, and careful licensing.</p>'''+source)
inject(14,'Licensing, clinical validation, and evidence limits', '''<p>The adapter release is <b>CC BY-NC 4.0</b>, which is non-commercial and not an OSI open-source license. Base-model licenses apply separately. The models are research artifacts, not medical devices. The reported training notes and tutorial records are synthetic, and the project has not clinically validated these workflows on real patient data. Published exam results may also be affected by base-model pretraining exposure.</p><p>Every performance figure in this guide is attributed to the project and model cards; no weights were downloaded and no full benchmark was rerun. Our executable test only validates the mathematical reconstruction of letter scoring and aggregation.</p>'''+source)
# Update source links and page descriptors
for tag in s.select('.metadata span'):
 if '14 chapters' in tag.text:tag.string='14 chapters · source-audited'
for tag in s.select('a[href="#sources"]'): pass
s.select_one('.lead').string='A source-audited technical reconstruction of MedDecider: published model variants, data recipes, letter-logit mathematics, order averaging, calibration, clinical use cases, benchmark evidence and executable verification.'
p.write_text(str(s),encoding='utf-8')
print('upgraded',p,'bytes',p.stat().st_size)
